NNAAMMEE
     kkiillll — terminate or signal a process

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     kkiillll [--_s_i_g_n_a_l___n_u_m_b_e_r | --_s_i_g_n_a_l___n_a_m_e | --ss _s_i_g_n_a_l___n_a_m_e] _p_i_d _._._.
     kkiillll --ll [_e_x_i_t___s_t_a_t_u_s]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The kkiillll utility sends a signal to the process(es) specified by the _p_i_d
     operand(s).  If no signal is specified, the TTEERRMM signal is used.

     Only the superuser may send signals to other users' processes.

     The options are as follows:

     --ll [_e_x_i_t___s_t_a_t_u_s]
             Display the name of the signal corresponding to _e_x_i_t___s_t_a_t_u_s.
             _e_x_i_t___s_t_a_t_u_s may be the exit status of a command killed by a sig‐
             nal (see the special sh(1) parameter ‘?’) or a signal number.

             If no operand is given, display the names of all the signals.

     --_s_i_g_n_a_l___n_u_m_b_e_r | --_s_i_g_n_a_l___n_a_m_e | --ss _s_i_g_n_a_l___n_a_m_e
             A non-negative decimal integer or a symbolic name specifying the
             signal to be sent instead of the default TTEERRMM.

     The following PIDs have special meanings:

           -1      If superuser, broadcast the signal to all processes; other‐
                   wise, broadcast to all processes belonging to the user.

           0       Send the signal to all processes whose group ID is equal to
                   the process group ID of the sender, and for which the
                   process has permission.

           -_p_g_i_d   Send the signal to all processes within the specified
                   process group.

     Some of the more commonly used signals:

           1     HHUUPP     (hang up)
           2     IINNTT     (interrupt)
           3     QQUUIITT    (quit)
           6     AABBRRTT    (abort)
           9     KKIILLLL    (non-catchable, non-ignorable kill)
           14    AALLRRMM    (alarm clock)
           15    TTEERRMM    (software termination signal)

     For a complete list, consult the signal(3) manual page.

     A signal number of 0 (kkiillll --00 _p_i_d) does not send a signal, but only
     checks the validity of a certain PID.  It succeeds if _p_i_d exists or
     raises an error otherwise.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     The kkiillll utility exits 0 on success, and >0 if an error occurs.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     Forcibly terminate process ID 1234:

           $ kill -9 1234

     Send the init(8) process the hangup signal, instructing it to re-read
     ttys(5):

           # kill -HUP 1

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     csh(1), ksh(1), pkill(1), ps(1), kill(2), signal(3)

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
     The kkiillll utility is compliant with the IEEE Std 1003.1-2008 (“POSIX.1”)
     specification.

     The --_s_i_g_n_a_l___n_a_m_e and --_s_i_g_n_a_l___n_u_m_b_e_r syntax is marked by IEEE Std
     1003.1-2008 (“POSIX.1”) as being an X/Open System Interfaces option.

     kkiillll also exists as a built-in to csh(1) and ksh(1), though with a dif‐
     ferent syntax.

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     A kkiillll command appeared in Version 3 AT&T UNIX.

